INSERT INTO vector_chunks_backup_20260327 VALUES(435,'art_movements_timeline.md',1,'','humanities_arts',replace('## Western Art Movements — Chronological Timeline\n\n| Movement | Period | Key Region | Defining Works | Auction Peak ($M) |\n|----------|--------|------------|----------------|------------------|\n| Early Renaissance | 1300–1490 | Florence, Italy | Masaccio frescoes, Ghiberti doors | $25–50 |\n| High Renaissance | 1490–1527 | Rome, Florence | Sistine Chapel (1508–1512), Mona Lisa | $450+ |\n| Mannerism | 1520–1600 | Italy, Spain | El Greco portraits | $5–20 |\n| Baroque | 1600–1750 | Italy, Flanders, Spain | Night Watch (1642), Velázquez | $20–60 |\n| Rococo | 1720–1780 | France | Watteau, Fragonard, Boucher | $5–15 |\n| Neoclassicism | 1760–1850 | France, UK | Jacques-Louis David, Ingres | $8–25 |\n| Romanticism | 1800–1850 | Germany, France, UK | Delacroix, Géricault, Turner | $15–50 | $10–18 |\n| Realism | 1840–1880 | France | Courbet, Millet, Daumier | $5–20 |\n| Impressionism | 1860–1886 | France | Monet ($110M), Renoir, Degas | $30–110 |\n| Post-Impressionism | 1886–1910 | France, Netherlands | Van Gogh ($149M), Cézanne ($60M) | $60–170 |\n| Symbolism | 1886–1910 | France, Belgium | Gustave Moreau, Odilon Redon | $2–10 |\n| Fauvism | 1905–1910 | France | Matisse ($86M), Derain | $15–90 |\n| Expressionism | 1905–1930 | Germany | Kirchner, Nolde, Schiele | $15–40 |\n| Cubism | 1907–1935 | France, Spain | Picasso ($179M), Braque | $30–180 |\n| Futurism | 1909–1944 | Italy | Umberto Boccioni, Severini | $2–12 |\n| Dada | 1916–1924 | Zurich, NYC | Marcel Duchamp, Man Ray | $5–15 |\n| Surrealism | 1924–1960 | France | Dalí ($42M), Magritte, Ernst | $20–100 |\n| Abstract Expressionism | 1940–1965 | USA | Pollock ($200M), Rothko ($186M) | $50–200 |\n| Pop Art | 1955–1975 | USA, UK | Warhol ($195M), Lichtenstein ($165M) | $50–200 |\n| Minimalism | 1960–1980 | USA | Carl Andre, Donald Judd | $5–50 |\n| Conceptual Art | 1965– | International | Sol LeWitt, Joseph Kosuth | $1–20 |\n| Neo-Expressionism | 1980–1995 | Germany, USA | Basquiat ($110M), Kiefer, Schnabel | $10–115 |\n| Contemporary | 1990– | Global | Hirst, Koons ($91M), Banksy | $1–100+ | $12–18 |','\n',char(10)),'2026-03-25 17:19:23');
INSERT INTO vector_chunks_backup_20260327 VALUES(436,'art_movements_timeline.md',2,'','humanities_arts',replace('## Key Art Prizes & Awards (2025)\n\n| Prize | Amount | Country | Focus | Recent Winner |\n|-------|--------|---------|-------|---------------|\n| Turner Prize | £25,000 | UK | Contemporary British art | Rory Pilgrim (2024) |\n| Pritzker Architecture Prize | $100,000 | Global | Architecture | Motoaki Matsuzaki (2025) |\n| Turner Prize (group) | £25,000 total | UK | — | — |\n| Hugo Boss Prize | $100,000 | USA | Contemporary art | Biennial |\n| Guggenheim Fellowship | ~$50,000 | USA | Visual arts, humanities | Annual ~175 fellows |\n| MacArthur Fellowship | $800,000 | USA | All disciplines | Annual ~20 fellows |\n| Wolf Prize (Arts) | $100,000 | Israel | Architecture, music, painting | Annual |','\n',char(10)),'2026-03-25 17:19:23');
INSERT INTO vector_chunks_backup_20260327 VALUES(437,'art_movements_timeline.md',3,'','humanities_arts',replace('## Literature Movements & Periods\n\n| Period | Dates | Key Works | Dominant Style | Edition ($) |\n|--------|-------|-----------|----------------|-------------|\n| Ancient / Classical | 800 BCE–400 CE | Iliad, Odyssey (~750 BCE), Aeneid (19 BCE) | Epic, tragedy, comedy | $10–20 |\n| Medieval | 500–1400 | Divine Comedy (1320), Canterbury Tales (1390) | Religious allegory, courtly love | $12–25 |\n| Renaissance | 1400–1600 | Don Quixote (1605), Shakespeare (1590–1613) | Humanism, secular themes | $10–20 |\n| Baroque | 1600–1700 | Paradise Lost (1667), Molière (1660–1673) | Ornate, dramatic | $12–22 |\n| Enlightenment | 1700–1800 | Candide (1759), Tom Jones (1749) | Reason, satire, social critique | $10–18 |\n| Romanticism | 1800–1850 | Frankenstein (1818), Les Misérables (1862) | Emotion, nature, individualism | $10–18 |\n| Realism | 1850–1900 | Anna Karenina (1877), Middlemarch (1871) | Social observation, detail | $10–16 |\n| Modernism | 1890–1940 | Ulysses (1922), Mrs Dalloway (1925), Wasteland (1922) | Stream of consciousness | $12–20 |\n| Postmodernism | 1960– | Catch-22 (1961), Gravity''s Rainbow (1973) | Metafiction, fragmentation | $12–20 |\n| Contemporary | 1990– | The Kite Runner (2003), Lincoln in the Bardo (2017) | Diverse, global voices | $12–18 |','\n',char(10)),'2026-03-25 17:19:23');
INSERT INTO vector_chunks_backup_20260327 VALUES(438,'art_movements_timeline.md',4,'','humanities_arts',replace('## Philosophy — Key Works with Dates\n\n| Work | Author | Year | Tradition | Pages (approx.) | Price ($) |\n|------|--------|------|-----------|-----------------|-----------|\n| Nicomachean Ethics | Aristotle | 349 BCE | Virtue Ethics | 300 | $9–15 |\n| Meditations | Marcus Aurelius | 161–180 CE | Stoicism | 140 | $7–12 |\n| Meditations on First Philosophy | Descartes | 1641 | Rationalism | 90 | $8–14 |\n| Leviathan | Hobbes | 1651 | Political Philosophy | 600 | $12–22 |\n| Ethics | Spinoza | 1677 | Rationalism | 250 | $10–18 |\n| An Essay Concerning Human Understanding | Locke | 1689 | Empiricism | 600 | $14–22 |\n| A Treatise of Human Nature | Hume | 1739 | Empiricism | 700 | $12–20 |\n| Critique of Pure Reason | Kant | 1781 | Idealism | 900 | $14–22 |\n| Phenomenology of Spirit | Hegel | 1807 | Idealism | 500 | $15–25 |\n| The World as Will and Representation | Schopenhauer | 1818 | Pessimism | 700 | $14–22 |\n| Beyond Good and Evil | Nietzsche | 1886 | Existentialism | 250 | $10–16 |\n| Being and Time | Heidegger | 1927 | Phenomenology | 600 | $18–28 |\n| Being and Nothingness | Sartre | 1943 | Existentialism | 700 | $16–25 |\n| Philosophical Investigations | Wittgenstein | 1953 | Analytic | 300 | $14–22 |\n| A Theory of Justice | Rawls | 1971 | Political Philosophy | 600 | $18–28 |','\n',char(10)),'2026-03-25 17:19:23');
INSERT INTO vector_chunks_backup_20260327 VALUES(439,'art_movements_timeline.md',5,'','humanities_arts',replace('## Data Quality Classification\n\n### Category A — Stable Historical Data:\n- Art movement dates and key works: Grove Art Online, Oxford Art History\n- Art prizes amounts: Turner Prize (tate.org.uk), Pritzker (pritzkerprize.com), MacArthur (macfound.org)\n- Literature period dates and key works: Norton Anthology of Literature, standard academic consensus\n- Philosophy works and dates: Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy (plato.stanford.edu)\n- Page counts: publisher data, commonly available editions\n\n### Category B — Estimates (⚠ directional only):\n- "Auction Peak ($M)" per movement: rough order-of-magnitude based on known record sales within each period.\n  Not from a single authoritative source; use only for ballpark comparison.\n- Edition prices ($): approximate contemporary retail prices; vary widely.','\n',char(10)),'2026-03-25 17:19:23');
INSERT INTO vector_chunks_backup_20260327 VALUES(440,'ethical_frameworks_comparison.md',0,'','humanities_arts',replace('---\ndomain: humanities_arts\nagent: philosophy-specialist\nkeywords_es: [ética, utilitarismo, deontología, virtud, dilema, moral, justicia, Kant, Rawls, trolley, imperativo categórico, contractualismo, cuidado]\nkeywords_en: [ethics, utilitarianism, deontology, virtue, dilemma, moral, justice, Kant, Rawls, trolley, categorical imperative, contractualism, care ethics]\n---\n\n# Ethical Frameworks — Comparative Reference','\n',char(10)),'2026-03-25 17:19:23');
INSERT INTO vector_chunks_backup_20260327 VALUES(441,'ethical_frameworks_comparison.md',1,'','humanities_arts',replace('## Framework Comparison Table\n\n| Framework | Core Principle | Decision Criterion | Key Strength | Key Weakness | Primary Thinkers |\n|-----------|---------------|-------------------|--------------|--------------|-----------------|\n| Act Utilitarianism | Maximize aggregate happiness (hedons) | Does this act produce the greatest good for the greatest number? | Quantifiable; democratic; flexible | Justifies harming minorities; requires impossible knowledge of all consequences | Bentham, early Mill |\n| Rule Utilitarianism | Follow rules that maximize utility | Would following this rule generally maximize utility? | Avoids act-utilitarianism''s extreme conclusions; stable expectations | Devolves into act-utilitarianism under pressure | Mill (later), Brandt |\n| Kantian Deontology | Act from duty according to universal law | Can I universalize this maxim without contradiction? | Protects individual rights absolutely; consistent | Rigid; conflicting duties unresolvable; ignores consequences entirely | Kant |\n| W.D. Ross''s Prima Facie Duties | Multiple duties, not one supreme principle | Which duty is most binding given ALL duties in this situation? | More flexible than Kant; intuitively plausible | No systematic resolution when duties conflict | W.D. Ross |\n| Virtue Ethics | Cultivate character to be a good person | What would a virtuous (phronimos) person do? | Holistic; contextual; focuses on character development | No action-guidance algorithm; who defines virtues? culturally variable | Aristotle, MacIntyre, Foot |\n| Care Ethics | Maintain caring relationships and respond to vulnerability | Does this preserve relationships and attend to the particular needs of those involved? | Contextual; relational; challenges impartiality assumption | Hard to scale beyond personal relationships; can justify partiality | Gilligan, Noddings, Held |\n| Contractarianism | Rules rational self-interested agents would agree to | Would self-interested rational agents agree to this rule? | Connects morality to rational self-interest | Ignores those outside the contract (animals, future generations, disabled) | Hobbes, Gauthier |\n| Contractualism | Principles no one could reasonably reject | Could this principle be justified to everyone affected? | Non-aggregative; respects each person | "Reasonable rejection" undefined; vague threshold | Rawls, Scanlon |\n| Natural Law | Morality derived from human nature/reason | Does this accord with our natural end (telos) and right reason? | Objective foundation; universal applicability claimed | Naturalistic fallacy risk; requires contested metaphysics | Aquinas, Finnis |','\n',char(10)),'2026-03-25 17:19:23');
INSERT INTO vector_chunks_backup_20260327 VALUES(442,'ethical_frameworks_comparison.md',2,'','humanities_arts',replace('## Kant''s Categorical Imperative — Three Formulations\n\n| Formulation | Statement | Test |\n|------------|-----------|------|\n| Universal Law | "Act only according to that maxim whereby you can at the same time will that it should become a universal law" | Would universalizing this maxim produce a contradiction? |\n| Humanity Formula | "Act so that you treat humanity, whether in your own person or in that of another, always as an end and never as a means only" | Am I using a person purely instrumentally? |\n| Kingdom of Ends | "Act according to maxims of a universally legislating member of a merely possible kingdom of ends" | Could rational agents agree to this rule in an ideal community? |','\n',char(10)),'2026-03-25 17:19:23');
INSERT INTO vector_chunks_backup_20260327 VALUES(443,'ethical_frameworks_comparison.md',3,'','humanities_arts',replace('## Rawls'' Theory of Justice — Key Elements\n\n```\nOriginal Position: hypothetical choice situation\nVeil of Ignorance: don''t know your place in society (class, race, gender, talents, conception of good)\n\nTwo Principles of Justice (lexical order):\n1. Equal Basic Liberties Principle: each person has equal basic liberties compatible with same liberties for all\n2. Difference Principle: social/economic inequalities must be:\n   (a) attached to offices/positions open to all under fair equality of opportunity\n   (b) arranged to benefit the least-advantaged members of society (maximin strategy)\n\nPriority rule: Principle 1 > Principle 2(a) > Principle 2(b)\nCritique (Nozick): Difference principle violates property rights; entitlement theory alternative\n```','\n',char(10)),'2026-03-25 17:19:23');
INSERT INTO vector_chunks_backup_20260327 VALUES(444,'ethical_frameworks_comparison.md',4,'','humanities_arts',replace('## Applied to Classic Dilemmas\n\n### Trolley Problem (runaway trolley, 5 vs 1)\n\n| Framework | Verdict | Reasoning |\n|-----------|---------|-----------|\n| Act Utilitarianism | Pull lever (5 > 1) | maximize aggregate welfare |\n| Kantian Deontology | DO NOT pull (contested) | using 1 person as means; or: duty to minimize harm (Ross: conflicting duties) |\n| Virtue Ethics | Contextual; what would a person of practical wisdom do? | most virtue ethicists: pull lever; focus on agent''s motive |\n| Care Ethics | Who has relationships? Who is more vulnerable? | resists abstract calculation; focuses on particular relationships |\n| Contractualism | Pull lever is permissible; nobody could reasonably reject the principle | Scanlon: but Footbridge variant (push fat man) is impermissible |\n\n**Trolley vs Footbridge distinction (Thomson 1985):** physically pushing someone activates different intuitions than redirecting harm via lever; documented across cultures in empirical moral psychology.\n\n### Heinz Dilemma (steal drug to save wife)\n\n| Framework | Verdict |\n|-----------|---------|\n| Utilitarianism | Steal: wife''s life > drug company''s profit |\n| Deontology (Kant) | Do not steal (cannot universalize theft); but also duty to preserve life → unresolvable |\n| Virtue Ethics | A compassionate person steals; justice requires facing consequences after |\n| Care Ethics | Steal: relationship to wife grounds the obligation |\n| Contractualism | Drug company cannot reasonably reject a principle permitting theft under life-threatening necessity |\n\nKohlberg used this dilemma to measure moral development stages: pre-conventional → conventional → post-conventional.\n\n### Corporate Whistleblowing\n\n| Framework | Verdict |\n|-----------|---------|\n| Utilitarianism | Blow whistle if public harm > personal/company harm; requires estimating probabilities |\n| Deontology | Duty of loyalty (contractual) vs duty to prevent harm; prima facie duty to prevent serious harm generally overrides |\n| Virtue Ethics | An honest, courageous person speaks truth; integrity demands it |\n| Care Ethics | To whom do you owe care? Colleagues at risk vs the public; particular relationships complicate the calculus |\n| Contractualism | Nobody could reasonably reject a principle requiring disclosure of serious public harm |\n\n**Source:** Rachels & Rachels "The Elements of Moral Philosophy" 9th ed. + Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy + Rawls "A Theory of Justice" revised ed. (1999)','\n',char(10)),'2026-03-25 17:19:23');
